-- title:
--   Remark 4.6, (4.10) — Markovian controls achieve the relaxed value: $\sup_{\beta\in\mathcal A_U}J_W(\mu,\beta)=\sup_{P\in\mathcal R}\langle P,\Gamma^\mu\rangle$
-- statement:
--   Assume the standing assumptions of §3. Let $W\in L^1_+[0,1]^2$, let $\lambda\in\mathcal P_{\mathrm{Unif}}([0,1]\times\mathbb R^d)$ be the initial law, and let $\mu_\cdot\in C([0,T];\mathcal P_{\mathrm{Unif}}([0,1]\times\mathbb R^d))$ be a measure flow. Then
--   $$\sup_{\beta\in\mathcal A_U}J_W(\mu,\beta)=\sup_{P\in\mathcal R}\langle P,\Gamma^\mu\rangle.\tag{4.10}$$
--
--   Here $J_W(\mu,\beta)$ is the graphon-game reward of the Markovian control $\beta(t,U,X_t)$, $\mathcal R$ is the set of laws on the canonical space solving the controlled martingale problem with initial law $\lambda$, and $\Gamma^\mu$ is the relaxed reward (4.1). That is, Markovian controls achieve the same value as the more general relaxed controls, which may depend on additional randomness.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: "for each μ", μ ∈ P_Unif([0,1] × C^d), entering through its time marginals μₜ → every continuous flow μ· ∈ C([0,T]; P_Unif([0,1] × ℝᵈ)); reason: the marginal flow of any such μ is continuous, and J_W and Γ^μ depend on μ only through it (4.9); specializes to the printed statement
--   - encoding: the two suprema → suprema in EReal; the left one over β ∈ 𝒜_U and over all solutions of (3.4) under β; reason: no junk value at an empty index set; solutions are unique in law
--   - reading: "σ is Lipschitz" (p.6) → σ(t,·) Lipschitz in x with one constant for all t ∈ [0,T] (entrywise); reason: the weaker of the two readings, hence the stronger theorem; well-posedness via [49, Theorem 1] (pp.7–8) needs only this
--   - encoding: standing assumptions of §3 (p.6): b, σ, f, g bounded continuous on [0,T] × ⋯ → Data.Standing: ContinuousOn and bounded on Iic T × ⋯, M₊(ℝᵈ) = FiniteMeasure with the weak topology; reason: the functions are only used on [0,T]
--   - encoding: d, d₀ ∈ ℕ, T > 0 (p.6) → 1 ≤ d and 0 < T; d₀ ≥ 1 is not stated separately; reason: the paper's ℕ starts at 1; d₀ ≥ 1 follows from σσᵀ ≥ c·I with c > 0 and d ≥ 1
--   - encoding: "the unique solution" of the state SDE (pp.7–8, 21) → solutions are a relation (GSol / NSol: a filtered probability space with an F-Brownian motion and a continuous adapted state satisfying the Itô equation); statements quantify over all solutions; reason: under the standing assumptions solutions exist and are unique in law ([49] cited p.7), so every payoff equals the paper's
--   - encoding: a given filtered probability space (Ω, F, 𝔽, P) (p.8), B¹,…,Bⁿ independent Brownian motions (p.7) → any probability space with a filtration for which the Brownian motion(s) are adapted with increments independent of the past; filtration not augmented; Itô integrals in the sense of Peng1990.SMP.IsItoIntegral; reason: payoffs depend only on the law of the solution, which is unique in law
--   - encoding: time t ∈ [0,T], labels u ∈ [0,1], ℝᵈ, C^d = C([0,T];ℝᵈ) → time ℝ≥0 read on [0,T] (paths clamped at T), labels in ℝ, ℝᵈ = Fin d → ℝ (sup norm), C^d = C(Icc 0 T, Fin d → ℝ) with its Borel σ-field; reason: Lean conventions; only t ∈ [0,T] and u ∈ [0,1] enter, and the norms on ℝᵈ give the same topology
--   - encoding: measurable controls on [0,T] × [0,1] × ℝᵈ (𝒜_U), [0,T] × ℝᵈ (𝒜₁), [0,T] × (ℝᵈ)ⁿ (𝒜ₙ) → measurable maps on ℝ≥0 × ℝ × ℝᵈ, ℝ≥0 × ℝᵈ, ℝ≥0 × (ℝᵈ)ⁿ; reason: every control on the smaller domain extends measurably; values outside never enter
--   - encoding: 𝐖m(u) ∈ M₊(E) (2.6) → Wop W m u, set to 0 at the labels u where ∫W(u,v)m(dv,dx) = ∞; reason: for m with uniform first marginal these labels form a Lebesgue-null set (Fubini, p.15), which no expectation over a uniform label sees
--   - encoding: 𝒱, the canonical filtration, "P-martingale", C_c^∞(ℝᵈ), ∇²φ (§4.2) → finite measures on ℝ × A with first marginal Leb|[0,T]; σ-fields generated by Λ(B), B ⊆ [0,t] × A, U and Xₛ, s ≤ t; the martingale identity on [0,T] stated directly; ContDiff ℝ ∞ with compact support; second Fréchet derivative; reason: Lean renderings of the same objects
-- source:
--   Lacker, Soret, A Label-State Formulation of Stochastic Graphon Games and Approximate Equilibria on Large Networks, arXiv:2204.09642v1, Remark 4.6, (4.10), p.20 (objects of §4.2, p.16)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GraphonLabelGame_Relaxed

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology Set
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace GraphonLabelGame

/-- **Remark 4.6, (4.10)**. Lacker–Soret, arXiv:2204.09642v1, Remark 4.6, (4.10), p.20.
Under the standing assumptions, for `W ∈ L¹₊[0, 1]²`, an initial law `λ ∈ P_Unif([0, 1] × ℝᵈ)`
and a measure flow `μ· ∈ C([0, T]; P_Unif([0, 1] × ℝᵈ))`,
`sup_{β ∈ 𝒜_U} J_W(μ, β) = sup_{P ∈ ℛ} ⟨P, Γ^μ⟩`.

**Formalization Note** Both suprema are taken in `EReal`. The left one runs over `β ∈ 𝒜_U` and
over all solutions of (3.4) under `β` (they are unique in law). The remark is printed "for each
`μ`" with `μ ∈ P_Unif([0, 1] × C^d)`, entering only through its time marginals; here it is
stated for every continuous flow, which contains every marginal flow of such a `μ`. -/
theorem remark_4_6 {d d₀ : ℕ} {A : Type*} [MetricSpace A] [CompactSpace A] [MeasurableSpace A]
    [BorelSpace A]
    (D : Data d d₀ A) (hD : D.Standing) (W : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) (hW : IsKernelL1Plus W)
    (lam : ProbabilityMeasure (ℝ × (Fin d → ℝ)))
    (hlam : IsUnifFirst (lam : Measure (ℝ × (Fin d → ℝ))))
    (μ : ℝ≥0 → ProbabilityMeasure (ℝ × (Fin d → ℝ))) (hμ : IsUnifFlow D.T μ) :
    (⨆ (β : ℝ≥0 → ℝ → (Fin d → ℝ) → A) (_ : IsUControl β)
        (S : GSol D β (lam : Measure (ℝ × (Fin d → ℝ)))), ((S.payoff W μ : ℝ) : EReal)) =
      ⨆ (P : ProbabilityMeasure (CanSpace d D.T A))
        (_ : P ∈ admissibleLaws D (lam : Measure (ℝ × (Fin d → ℝ)))),
        ((∫ ω, Gamma D W μ ω ∂(P : Measure (CanSpace d D.T A)) : ℝ) : EReal) := by sorry

end GraphonLabelGame
